1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
We eat over 10 billion pounds of it each year.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:12,000
It satiated the ancients and built modern-day empires,

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
from the equatorial fields into famous factories,

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
from bonbons to bunnies, pods to peanut butter cups.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:22,000
At the heart of this giant industry

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
is a little magic bean believed to be an energizer,

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
aphrodisiac, and cure-all.

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,000
Now, Chocolate on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:45,000
Bars.

10
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:46,000
Ice cream.

11
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:48,000
Cookies.

12
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:50,000
Cake.

13
00:00:50,001 --> 00:00:51,000
Candy.

14
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:52,000
Santas.

15
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:53,000
Hearts.

16
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:54,000
Bunnies.

17
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:55,000
Eggs.

18
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:58,000
Hollow, filled, hot.

19
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:02,040
cold, solid, liquid, powder.

20
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:05,940
Chocolate comes in many forms and has legions of fans.

21
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:15,760
Annual sales in the US top $16 billion and $75 billion

22
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:16,660
worldwide.

23
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,360
The average American consumes 12 pounds per year.

24
00:01:24,361 --> 00:01:27,960
The sweet toothed Swiss, an astounding 22 pounds

25
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:29,460
per person each year.

26
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:35,640
Millions of people engage in the production of chocolate

27
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:38,880
so that millions more can revel in their passion for it.

28
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:47,220
One of the companies that capitalizes on this passion

29
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:51,880
is Mars Incorporated, maker of M&Ms.

30
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:54,360
Welcome to the M&Ms factory at the University of America.

31
00:01:54,361 --> 00:01:57,660
in Hackettstown, New Jersey.

32
00:01:57,661 --> 00:01:59,760
And all we make here is M&Ms.

33
00:01:59,761 --> 00:02:01,760
We have several varieties of M&Ms.

34
00:02:01,761 --> 00:02:03,660
We have milk chocolate.

35
00:02:03,661 --> 00:02:05,700
We used to call it plain.

36
00:02:05,701 --> 00:02:07,140
But that seemed too simple.

37
00:02:07,141 --> 00:02:08,380
We have peanut.

38
00:02:08,381 --> 00:02:09,700
We have almond.

39
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:13,540
And we have peanut butter.

40
00:02:13,541 --> 00:02:15,840
And they produce several million of them per day.

41
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:23,020
The process begins as milk chocolate

42
00:02:23,021 --> 00:02:26,860
and blended for the optimal flavor, texture, and melting

43
00:02:26,861 --> 00:02:28,360
point.

44
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:29,860
We've made the chocolate.

45
00:02:29,861 --> 00:02:31,360
Now it's time to make the M&Ms.

46
00:02:31,361 --> 00:02:32,860
And this is the first step.

47
00:02:32,861 --> 00:02:36,360
The chocolate is being deposited on chilled rollers.

48
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:38,560
Each of those rollers have dimples on them.

49
00:02:38,561 --> 00:02:40,980
So the chocolate's being pressed into it.

50
00:02:40,981 --> 00:02:44,980
And the output is a roll of dimples that go through a

51
00:02:44,981 --> 00:02:47,980
cooling tunnel and on into the next step of production.

52
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:52,980
The cooled chocolate pellets are transferred

53
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:57,160
to these giant rotating pans, where they roll against each

54
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:00,220
other and start to take on the familiar shape prior

55
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:04,860
to the application of the signature outer coating.

56
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:08,340
Each of these pans hold about 1,000 pounds.

57
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:11,840
And what happens is that you start to see the beginning

58
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:13,300
of an M&M.

59
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,100
This is an opportunity for us to do a quality check.

60
00:03:16,101 --> 00:03:17,000
check.

61
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:18,100
We're looking at the weight.

62
00:03:18,101 --> 00:03:19,440
We're looking at the size.

63
00:03:19,441 --> 00:03:22,740
As we go into the sugar shell, you have

64
00:03:22,741 --> 00:03:25,700
to have a uniform M&M.

65
00:03:25,701 --> 00:03:28,580
A mixture made with liquid sucrose, cornstarch,

66
00:03:28,581 --> 00:03:32,580
and coloring is poured into the pans, enveloping each pellet

67
00:03:32,581 --> 00:03:33,380
as it hardens.

68
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:42,300
Next, the sugar-coated candy travels to the printing area.

69
00:03:42,301 --> 00:03:44,300
Each piece sits snugly in its slot,

70
00:03:44,301 --> 00:03:49,340
under a printing roller, bearing the signature M

71
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:50,840
in an edible white dye.

72
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:56,340
Mars can even custom print your M&Ms with everything

73
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:59,320
from marriage proposals to the logo of your favorite TV

74
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:00,120
network.

75
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:07,460
Finally, individual conveyors carrying a single color of candy

76
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:10,320
dump onto a central belt on the road to packaging.

77
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:17,260
But no matter what's on the shelf,

78
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,040
it's the chocolate inside that's the real treat.

79
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:28,240
All chocolate starts life as a seed, or bean,

80
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:32,160
inside the fruit of the cacao tree.

81
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:33,960
There are three kinds of cocoa beans.

82
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:39,300
There are the criollos that were originated in New Venezuela

83
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,220
and cultivated in Mesoamerica.

84
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:43,500
The criollos have a very good flavor,

85
00:04:43,501 --> 00:04:48,040
but they may not be very sturdy, very aggressive,

86
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:50,340
or resistant to diseases.

87
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:52,740
Then we have the Amazonicos, or the Forasteros,

88
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:56,140
that are very sturdy, very resistant, very highly

89
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:57,140
productive.

90
00:04:57,240 --> 00:04:59,440
And there is a mixture of these two that is called Trinitarian.

91
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:04,260
The cacao tree grows primarily

92
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:07,500
in regions within 10 degrees north or south of the equator.

93
00:05:07,501 --> 00:05:11,800
later.

94
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:15,200
It's a finicky plant that requires rich soil, steady

95
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:20,040
rainfall, and tropical heat, but also shade from the tropical

96
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:22,140
sun and shelter from the wind.

97
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:30,060
It takes five to six months for the cacao pods to ripen,

98
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:34,400
allowing for harvest twice a year.

99
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,000
Here in Costa Rica and around the world,

100
00:05:37,001 --> 00:05:41,240
Farmers harvest the pods by hand, a practice unchanged

101
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:44,000
for over 2,000 years.

102
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,340
Machines could damage the soil, delicate trees, and pods.

103
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:55,160
They put these pods in this pile,

104
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:59,560
and everybody sits around to break the pods.

105
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:00,560
Here you are.

106
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:04,660
These are the beans, and have this flesh, this pod,

107
00:06:04,661 --> 00:06:08,000
which is sweet and sour, very good for taste.

108
00:06:08,001 --> 00:06:09,500
You can eat it, you know?

109
00:06:09,501 --> 00:06:10,560
You can.

110
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:14,060
The pulp may be sweet, but the beans inside

111
00:06:14,061 --> 00:06:14,761
are quite bitter.

112
00:06:18,540 --> 00:06:23,800
An experienced worker can crack open up to 500 pods an hour.

113
00:06:23,801 --> 00:06:26,000
Each pod will yield about 30 to 40 beans.

114
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:33,720
Next, the beans need to ferment.

115
00:06:33,721 --> 00:06:36,220
If the farm isn't near a processing facility,

116
00:06:36,221 --> 00:06:37,760
workers use the traditional method

117
00:06:37,761 --> 00:06:41,360
of laying piles of beans between banana leaves

118
00:06:41,361 --> 00:06:44,960
to keep out dirt and rain, and to insulate against heat loss.

119
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:52,500
During the fermentation process, yeast

120
00:06:52,501 --> 00:06:54,800
metabolizes the sugars in the pulp,

121
00:06:54,801 --> 00:06:59,580
producing heat and ethanol as byproducts.

122
00:06:59,581 --> 00:07:02,320
Bacteria consume the ethanol and produce

123
00:07:02,321 --> 00:07:04,120
acids.

124
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:06,920
The alcohol and acids penetrate the shell,

125
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:10,120
and together with the heat create a reaction that allows

126
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:12,460
the cocoa flavor of the beans to develop,

127
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:14,460
and some of the bitterness to subside.

128
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:22,580
After fermenting for about six days,

129
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:24,880
the beans are ready to be dried in the sun.

130
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:37,320
Here we have the cacao beans drying in the sun.

131
00:07:37,321 --> 00:07:45,320
These have been drying for two days and need at least five more days to reach a moisture level of 7%.

132
00:07:45,321 --> 00:07:51,320
The advantage is that they will have a nice aroma and flavor.

133
00:07:55,320 --> 00:08:01,320
The less moisture in the beans, the less likely they are to grow mold during shipment.

134
00:08:01,321 --> 00:08:05,320
The dried beans are then scrutinized for quality,

135
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:08,320
bagged and weighed.

136
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:16,860
It takes roughly 1,600 to 1,700 pods, or 60,000 beans,

137
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:21,880
to fill up a bag that can cost anywhere from $150 to $800.

138
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,620
Now the sacks are ready to be shipped worldwide to chocolate

139
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:31,320
manufacturers.

140
00:08:31,321 --> 00:08:36,060
like Guitard chocolate in Burlingame, California.

141
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:38,360
Guitard provides chocolate in bulk quantities

142
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:41,160
to companies and professionals that will make the products

143
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:41,960
we all crave.

144
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:47,900
The products that a lot of our customers make

145
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,980
are candy bonbons or your traditional truffle.

146
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:53,780
Some people make candy bars.

147
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:57,680
Fine restaurants will make chocolate molten cakes

148
00:08:57,780 --> 00:08:59,880
and mousses.

149
00:08:59,881 --> 00:09:03,880
We use our chocolate chips for making cookies.

150
00:09:03,881 --> 00:09:06,880
Guittard creates chocolate formulas unique to each type

151
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:10,880
of product, and it all starts with a bean.

152
00:09:10,881 --> 00:09:14,880
Beans come to us from all over the world, from Africa,

153
00:09:14,881 --> 00:09:20,880
Central America, South America, and the Far East, and we select

154
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:23,880
the flavors that we're interested in making for each specific

155
00:09:23,881 --> 00:09:25,880
chocolate.

156
00:09:25,881 --> 00:09:30,880
Each region of the world produces a particular flavor.

157
00:09:30,881 --> 00:09:36,880
These beans are what we refer to as a raw agricultural commodity.

158
00:09:36,881 --> 00:09:41,880
Nobody on the face of the earth will ever put these into their mouth in this condition.

159
00:09:41,881 --> 00:09:49,880
They're dusty, there is stuff in them, and the first job is to clean these beans.

160
00:09:49,881 --> 00:09:55,720
The beans travel through a hopper to the cleaning area,

161
00:09:55,721 --> 00:09:59,260
where forced air, vibration, and then a vacuum

162
00:09:59,261 --> 00:10:02,760
will start to remove the foreign material.

163
00:10:02,761 --> 00:10:07,100
After we clean the beans, we come up to what we call

164
00:10:07,101 --> 00:10:09,600
the de-stoner.

165
00:10:09,601 --> 00:10:15,700
As the name implies, its job is to remove stones.

166
00:10:15,701 --> 00:10:20,700
Our milling equipment does not like rocks very much.

167
00:10:20,701 --> 00:10:24,700
It does a fair amount of damage, so this is critical.

168
00:10:24,701 --> 00:10:27,700
But there's one other piece.

169
00:10:27,701 --> 00:10:34,700
You have to remember that where beans are grown in the world are jungle conditions.

170
00:10:34,701 --> 00:10:39,700
This is a very wild place, including wild animals.

171
00:10:39,701 --> 00:10:42,700
You don't go out into the jungle unprotected.

172
00:10:42,701 --> 00:10:46,200
One of the things we pick up in this de-stoner

173
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:49,700
is we also pick up live ammunition.

174
00:10:49,701 --> 00:10:54,700
We consider it really good form to remove all live ammo

175
00:10:54,701 --> 00:10:59,700
before we roast the cocoa beans, for some fairly obvious reasons.

176
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:06,700
The cleaned, unloaded beans are loaded into the roaster.

177
00:11:06,701 --> 00:11:11,700
The roasting process can take anywhere from 10 to 30 minutes.

178
00:11:11,701 --> 00:11:14,600
From here, we have to remove the shell.

179
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:17,740
So it goes into a machine we call the winnower, which

180
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:20,940
breaks the bean apart, removes the shell,

181
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:23,540
and we are left with a pure nib.

182
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:24,840
This is the heart.

183
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:26,240
This is what we're really after.

184
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:33,160
The nibs are pre-milled into a coarse paste called

185
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:34,260
chocolate liquor.

186
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:41,700
Then onto the triple stone mill for finer grinding.

187
00:11:41,701 --> 00:11:46,700
The mill consists of three pairs of stone grinders.

188
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,800
Coarse chocolate liquor is pumped to the first stone pair.

189
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:54,240
The top stone rotates and grinds the liquor as it is forced

190
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:57,940
toward the outer edges and collected.

191
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:01,180
The mass flows down a chute to the next stone pair,

192
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:03,780
where it's ground even finer.

193
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:07,600
A third pass and then out the bottom.

194
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:11,620
The cocoa oil is a little bit more

195
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,940
particle size is now one-sixth the diameter of a human hair.

196
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:22,140
The resulting liquid chocolate liquor is divided.

197
00:12:22,240 --> 00:12:23,840
A portion goes to the cocoa press

198
00:12:23,940 --> 00:12:26,240
to have the cocoa butter literally squeezed out of it.

199
00:12:32,860 --> 00:12:35,300
Chocolate liquor is loaded into vertical pots

200
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:37,800
inside the cocoa press.

201
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:41,700
Using hydraulic pressure of up to 8,000 pounds PSI,

202
00:12:41,701 --> 00:12:45,160
The main ram slowly pushes pistons into each pot,

203
00:12:45,940 --> 00:12:46,720
compressing the liquor.

204
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:50,660
Most of the fat is released and collected below,

205
00:12:51,180 --> 00:12:53,920
and you're left with cocoa powder cakes.

206
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:57,860
We will grind it up, and it will become cocoa powder

207
00:12:57,861 --> 00:13:01,120
that you use in baking a cake or some brownies at home.

208
00:13:03,600 --> 00:13:06,040
Then it's back to the other portion of chocolate liquor

209
00:13:06,041 --> 00:13:07,740
that hasn't had its fat removed.

210
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:14,300
The liquor is combined with sugar or sugar and milk

211
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:16,800
powder, depending on the desired end product.

212
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:24,140
A series of steel rollers crushes and refines the thick mixture

213
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:25,440
into a flaky substance.

214
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:32,360
Then the conch, essentially a giant mixer,

215
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:35,760
helps distribute the fat and develop the texture

216
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:38,960
as the liquid ingredients are integrated.

217
00:13:38,961 --> 00:13:42,600
It typically will add somewhere around 28 to 32 pounds of

218
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:45,500
lecithin for 10,000 pounds of chocolate.

219
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:49,700
And then we'll add additional cocoa butter.

220
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:53,500
The soy lecithin acts as an emulsifier, helping the wet

221
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:55,280
and dry ingredients mix well.

222
00:13:57,840 --> 00:14:00,820
Incorporating the extra cocoa butter creates a less viscous

223
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:05,320
blend that will make the chocolate creamier.

224
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:08,960
From the conches, we're going to go to a set of tanks

225
00:14:08,961 --> 00:14:14,960
and from the tanks, we send it to be deposited as 10-pound bars,

226
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:20,960
as wafers, as cookie rocks, or possibly not to be deposited

227
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:24,960
at all, but to be sent out as a 50,000-pound liquid tanker

228
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:26,960
full of chocolate.

229
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:29,960
All this good stuff from processing a seed grown

230
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:30,960
in the rainforest.

231
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:38,940
This symbiosis of manpower and machine power has been

232
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:44,360
that satisfies a worldwide craving two millennia old,

233
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:46,760
a craving born not just of taste,

234
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:50,300
but of the basic desires for sex and money.

235
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:02,520
At Max Brenner Chocolate by the Bald Man in New York City,

236
00:15:02,620 --> 00:15:05,160
chocolate can be a refined affair

237
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:08,960
or an all-out gluttonous free-for-all.

238
00:15:08,961 --> 00:15:11,800
Chocolate pizza, anyone?

239
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:17,240
How about a syringe full of chocolate squirted right

240
00:15:17,340 --> 00:15:18,340
into your mouth?

241
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:21,700
Dieters, beware.

242
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:25,440
Max Brenner is creating a new chocolate culture.

243
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:30,380
We use this very special food ingredient, chocolate,

244
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:35,620
to give each person who comes here a very intimate and personal

245
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:37,280
experience.

246
00:15:37,281 --> 00:15:40,480
and personal experience.

247
00:15:40,580 --> 00:15:42,880
Renner's personal chocophilia

248
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:46,620
rivals that of historic fanatics.

249
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:51,120
If we look many years backwards to the time of the Aztec,

250
00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:54,040
the king of the Aztec, who was like the god,

251
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:59,240
he was allowed to eat this food because they said that it has

252
00:15:59,340 --> 00:16:01,840
all these very unique virtues.

253
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:07,280
Today, we can all be kings because we can all eat chocolate.

254
00:16:07,281 --> 00:16:11,420
The current state of chocoholism traces back to the Mesoamerican

255
00:16:11,421 --> 00:16:17,520
cultures, the classic Maya civilization of Central America

256
00:16:17,521 --> 00:16:22,520
and Southern Mexico, and the Aztecs of Central Mexico, and

257
00:16:22,521 --> 00:16:26,340
possibly even as far back as the Olmec civilization.

258
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:32,640
During ceremonies and celebrations, Maya and Aztec

259
00:16:32,641 --> 00:16:37,280
warriors, priests, and nobility drank a hot cup of tea.

260
00:16:37,281 --> 00:16:40,220
of ground-roasted cocoa beans and water,

261
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:41,820
to which spices were often added.

262
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:48,320
They called the bitter drink, chocolatl.

263
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:56,140
Soledad Lopez, of Aztec descent, uses old-world tools

264
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:58,940
and techniques to make chocolatl for her Los Angeles,

265
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:01,940
California restaurant.

266
00:17:01,941 --> 00:17:07,240
We picked the best cacaos possible.

267
00:17:07,340 --> 00:17:10,180
And then after that, we have to get it,

268
00:17:10,280 --> 00:17:15,880
and we roast it in the comal.

269
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:19,160
After 20 minutes of slow roasting over charcoal,

270
00:17:19,260 --> 00:17:21,220
the Aztecs peeled the darkened beans

271
00:17:21,320 --> 00:17:25,160
and painstakingly ground them 20 separate times

272
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:28,100
on a limestone matate.

273
00:17:28,101 --> 00:17:34,600
Usually it takes about eight hours,

274
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:38,880
and usually done only by women and little girls,

275
00:17:38,980 --> 00:17:42,540
because it has to be very patient.

276
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:46,180
The resulting thick paste was formed into measured cakes

277
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:49,620
and set aside to dry.

278
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:52,720
Three cakes are added per liter of hot water

279
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:54,820
and then crushed with a wood molineo.

280
00:17:58,100 --> 00:18:01,300
It was the frothy elixir of Aztec life,

281
00:18:01,400 --> 00:18:04,340
and sometimes a taste of the bitter end.

282
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:07,680
It had symbolic reference because the cacao pud

283
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,180
is sort of the shape of a human heart.

284
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:12,720
And of course, the Aztecs, we know,

285
00:18:12,820 --> 00:18:16,880
were enormously interested in heart extraction

286
00:18:16,980 --> 00:18:18,180
during sacrifices.

287
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:23,800
They were also interested in chocolate

288
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:26,620
as an aphrodisiac.

289
00:18:26,621 --> 00:18:30,820
Legend has it that Aztec ruler Montezuma consumed 50 goblets

290
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:34,860
of chocolatel per day, usually right before he

291
00:18:34,960 --> 00:18:36,760
entered his harem.

292
00:18:36,860 --> 00:18:38,360
Which is ridiculous.

293
00:18:38,460 --> 00:18:39,560
Nobody could do that.

294
00:18:39,660 --> 00:18:40,560
It's a diuretic.

295
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:44,280
He'd have to go to the bathroom very quickly because of the

296
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:47,280
caffeine and theobromane in it.

297
00:18:47,380 --> 00:18:50,380
So maybe 50 goblets a day isn't historically

298
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:53,120
accurate, but for centuries, people have believed that

299
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:56,620
chocolate puts you in the mood and increases the ability.

300
00:18:56,621 --> 00:18:57,820
stamina.

301
00:18:57,920 --> 00:19:02,020
It does contain chemicals that influence blood flow.

302
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:07,200
And certainly amorous activity and the redistribution of blood

303
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:10,160
in the body go together.

304
00:19:10,260 --> 00:19:13,100
And there are chemicals in chocolate

305
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:15,840
that likely improve mood as well.

306
00:19:15,940 --> 00:19:20,580
One of the likely candidates is an amino acid called tryptophan,

307
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:23,640
which is the precursor to a chemical in the brain

308
00:19:23,641 --> 00:19:27,380
called serotonin, which enhances mood.

309
00:19:27,480 --> 00:19:30,880
In any case, cocoa was valuable.

310
00:19:30,980 --> 00:19:34,580
The Maya and Aztecs even used the beans as currency.

311
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:36,880
A rabbit cost four to 30 beans.

312
00:19:36,980 --> 00:19:38,880
A mule, 50.

313
00:19:38,980 --> 00:19:41,900
A turkey or slave, 100 beans.

314
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,740
There is some debate over who brought cocoa beans and the

315
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:53,640
recipe for chocolatel back to Spain in the 16th century.

316
00:19:53,641 --> 00:19:59,480
Columbus, Cortez, or Mayan emissaries.

317
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:02,580
The Spanish added sugar to the bitter drink to appease their

318
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:04,880
delicate palates.

319
00:20:04,980 --> 00:20:07,720
A big hit with the country's elite, chocolate was a

320
00:20:07,820 --> 00:20:09,260
jealously guarded secret.

321
00:20:12,200 --> 00:20:16,060
The secret was so well kept, in fact, that 16th century English

322
00:20:16,160 --> 00:20:20,300
pirates who captured a Spanish treasure ship were perplexed to

323
00:20:20,301 --> 00:20:25,000
to find what looked like a cargo of dried sheep's droppings.

324
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:26,900
They burned the entire ship in anger.

325
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:31,540
Little did they know that in Spain,

326
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:33,540
the beans were worth their weight in silver.

327
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:40,260
It took nearly a century, but eventually the proverbial beans

328
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:42,760
were spilled, and everyone in Europe

329
00:20:42,860 --> 00:20:44,260
went mad for sweet chocolate.

330
00:20:44,261 --> 00:20:45,260
chocolate.

331
00:20:49,260 --> 00:20:53,600
To ensure a constant supply of sugar, and now cocoa,

332
00:20:53,700 --> 00:20:56,540
colonists built an ill-gotten labor force in the growing

333
00:20:56,640 --> 00:20:59,280
regions of Central America and the Caribbean.

334
00:20:59,380 --> 00:21:01,640
The native Indian peoples were dying off

335
00:21:01,740 --> 00:21:03,320
from introduced diseases.

336
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:06,380
They simply weren't resistant to smallpox

337
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:10,760
and other terrible European or Old World diseases.

338
00:21:10,860 --> 00:21:12,820
So they had a diminished labor force.

339
00:21:12,821 --> 00:21:16,720
To make up for this, they got into the African slave trade.

340
00:21:16,820 --> 00:21:20,220
And hundreds of thousands, eventually millions,

341
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:24,960
of Africans were torn out of Africa.

342
00:21:25,060 --> 00:21:27,960
Back in Europe, due to high import taxes,

343
00:21:28,060 --> 00:21:30,480
chocolate was hard on the wallet.

344
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:33,080
And due to the high fat content in the beans,

345
00:21:33,180 --> 00:21:34,480
it was hard on the stomach too.

346
00:21:38,520 --> 00:21:42,820
In 1828, a simple machine made it a bit easier to swallow.

347
00:21:42,821 --> 00:21:44,680
and digest.

348
00:21:46,760 --> 00:21:50,460
The cocoa press squeezed about half of the natural fat

349
00:21:50,461 --> 00:21:51,500
from the cocoa solids.

350
00:21:52,620 --> 00:21:55,520
The resulting cake could be pulverized into a powder

351
00:21:55,521 --> 00:21:57,800
and added to water or milk.

352
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:03,780
In 1847, Mr. Fry in England

353
00:22:03,781 --> 00:22:11,680
was able to produce a first solid, edible chocolate bar,

354
00:22:11,681 --> 00:22:16,680
Fry took the cocoa butter and added it with cocoa powder

355
00:22:16,780 --> 00:22:19,680
and sugar.

356
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:22,720
This recipe eliminated water from the equation,

357
00:22:22,820 --> 00:22:26,420
allowing the product to harden.

358
00:22:26,520 --> 00:22:29,740
The bar was bitter and brittle by today's standards,

359
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:32,540
but it was a start.

360
00:22:32,640 --> 00:22:34,140
Chocolate makers searched for ways

361
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:37,440
to make the bars more palatable.

362
00:22:37,540 --> 00:22:40,480
The answer was milk.

363
00:22:40,481 --> 00:22:43,220
But it didn't mix well with the cocoa powder, cocoa butter

364
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:45,520
combination.

365
00:22:45,620 --> 00:22:48,120
Because milk is nearly 90% water,

366
00:22:48,220 --> 00:22:52,560
it was kind of like mixing oil and water.

367
00:22:52,660 --> 00:22:56,620
In 1879, Swiss chocolatiers created the first milk

368
00:22:56,720 --> 00:23:00,140
chocolate by incorporating sweetened, evaporated milk.

369
00:23:02,740 --> 00:23:04,640
The 19th and early 20th centuries

370
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:09,140
saw a huge chocolate boom, with today's familiar company

371
00:23:09,141 --> 00:23:13,780
names like Cadbury, Lint, Hershey, and Mars

372
00:23:13,880 --> 00:23:14,840
coming into existence.

373
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:19,680
Luckily for these industry giants,

374
00:23:19,780 --> 00:23:22,180
the taste for the brown bean had spread,

375
00:23:22,280 --> 00:23:26,100
and consumer appetite was about to become insatiable.

376
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:37,240
We've been chocolate addicts since the very first bars

377
00:23:37,241 --> 00:23:39,200
hit stores in the mid-19th century.

378
00:23:42,540 --> 00:23:46,580
Today, one can find just about anything covered in chocolate.

379
00:23:46,680 --> 00:23:55,700
Fruit, nuts, nougat, pretzels, caramel, cookies, peanut butter,

380
00:23:55,800 --> 00:23:58,700
